-- allium: 3
-- voice-capture.allium

-- Scope: How spoken words live in Vera alongside what they say
-- Includes: A recording attached to a block, transcribing and transcribing
--   again, discarding the audio, the evidence a recording carries, and the
--   appellation of origin that survives everything done to the content
--   afterwards
-- Excludes:
--   - Capture, encoding and playback mechanics
--   - The accuracy and internal implementation of a particular speech model
--   - Speaker authentication, which does not exist yet (see identity-access.allium)
--   - How blocks are edited once they exist (see block-editing.allium)
--   - Storage of the recording itself (see content-media.allium)
-- Dependencies: Page, Block, Participant and OriginEvidence are governed by core.allium;
--   the recording is a MediaResource governed by content-media.allium; the
--   authorship that marks machine-written text by agent-participation.allium

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./block-editing.allium" as block

enum VoiceCapabilityState { disabled | incomplete | ready }

entity VoiceRuntimeConfiguration {
    owner: core/Participant
    enabled: Boolean
    speech_to_text_enabled: Boolean
    text_to_speech_enabled: Boolean
    speech_to_text_state: VoiceCapabilityState
    text_to_speech_state: VoiceCapabilityState
}

contract GovernedVoiceRuntime {
    configure: (owner: core/Participant, configuration: VoiceRuntimeConfiguration) -> VoiceRuntimeConfiguration

    @invariant VoiceIsOptional
        -- Vera remains usable when voice is disabled or no model is installed.
        -- Recording controls never imply that transcription or synthesis exists.

    @invariant ListeningAndSpeakingAreSeparateCapabilities
        -- Speech-to-text and text-to-speech have distinct switches, engines,
        -- models and diagnoses. Installing one never claims the other is ready.

    @invariant RequestedIsNotReady
        -- A switch records intent. A capability becomes ready only after its
        -- executable, model and supporting runtime are present and checked.
        -- Missing pieces stay visible as incomplete rather than a green state.

    @invariant InstallationRequiresTheOwner
        -- The browser never downloads or executes a model by itself. Vera may
        -- provide a pinned installation recipe, but an owner runs it with the
        -- authority of the host and grants the resulting paths to this runtime.

    @invariant ModelsLiveWithDurableInstanceData
        -- In a container, voice models and mutable caches live on the durable
        -- volume. Replacing the application image does not silently erase them.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Lo que hablar deja.
--
-- Una grabación pegada a un bloque, y el texto de ese bloque debajo. Nada más.
-- El audio se oye ahí mismo mientras exista, el texto se edita como cualquier
-- otro texto, y ninguna de las dos cosas consume a la otra.
--
-- Esto reemplaza una cascada de cuatro estados —grabado, transcrito, validado,
-- asentado— donde cada paso pedía una declaración y el último se llevaba el
-- audio por delante. La cascada protegía algo real: que el texto de una máquina
-- no se confundiera con lo que una persona dijo. Pero lo protegía con una
-- ceremonia, y una ceremonia se pulsa sin leer. Lo mismo queda mejor protegido
-- por la autoría, que no es una afirmación sobre quién está de acuerdo sino un
-- hecho sobre quién escribió; y por el audio, que se queda ahí para poder
-- comprobarlo.
contract SpokenContent {
    capture: (participant: core/Participant, block: core/Block, evidence: core/OriginEvidence) -> Recording
    transcribe: (recording: Recording) -> core/Block
    discard_audio: (participant: core/Participant, recording: Recording) -> Recording

    @invariant TheRecordingStaysUntilItIsDiscarded
        -- A recording is audible from where its words are read, for as long as it
        -- exists, and it exists until its owner says otherwise. Nothing consumes
        -- it: not transcribing, not editing what it said, not the completion of
        -- any workflow. What speaking leaves is a thing one keeps, and things one
        -- keeps do not disappear on their own.

    @invariant TheRecordingOutlivesItsTranscript
        -- Transcribing never replaces the recording, and rewriting the text never
        -- touches it. The recording is the head of the chain; a derivative cannot
        -- overwrite what it derives from. Transcribing again replaces the text,
        -- and only the text.

    @invariant TheMachineNeverPassesForAHand
        -- A transcript arrives written by the machine and says so, in the same
        -- authorship every block carries. It stops saying so when a person edits
        -- it, because then the words are theirs.
        --
        -- This is what a validation step used to declare, and it is better said
        -- here: it is a fact about who typed rather than a claim about who
        -- agrees, so it cannot be clicked through without reading.

    @invariant DiscardingIsAlwaysAvailableAndNeverImplied
        -- The audio can be deleted at any moment, transcribed or not, and it is
        -- deleted only when someone asks for exactly that. Refusing until a
        -- workflow completes would make Vera the owner of the recording; doing it
        -- as a side effect of something else would make it an accident. Both are
        -- worse than saying plainly what is about to be lost.

    @invariant SpokenContentNamesItsRecording
        -- A block that holds a recording names it. This is the appellation of
        -- origin: the block does not merely happen to have come from somewhere,
        -- it says where. It keeps saying it after the audio is discarded, because
        -- what was spoken was spoken.
}

contract AppellationOfOrigin {
    trace: (block: core/Block) -> Recording?

    @invariant OriginSurvivesEditing
        -- Rewriting a block does not change where it came from. What was spoken
        -- was spoken, whatever the words become afterwards.

    @invariant OriginSurvivesFragmentation
        -- Splitting a block leaves both halves naming the same recording, and
        -- merging two blocks of the same origin keeps it. Content divides; its
        -- provenance does not.

    @invariant OriginIsNeverInherited
        -- A block written by hand next to spoken content has no origin, and never
        -- acquires one by proximity. An appellation that spread by contact would
        -- be worth nothing.

    @invariant OriginIsNeverAsserted
        -- A block's origin comes from having held the recording, and from nowhere
        -- else. No participant and no agent can declare that a block came from a
        -- recording it did not come from.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Recording {
    graph: core/Graph

    -- The stored audio, addressed by the hash of its content. Null once
    -- discarded: the recording remains, and says its audio is gone rather than
    -- pretending it never existed.
    audio: String?
    media_type: String
    duration_ms: Integer?

    -- What the machine produced, the last time it was asked.
    --
    -- Kept beside the block and not instead of it: the block's text is what the
    -- text is now, and this is what the machine said. The difference between them
    -- is exactly what a person changed, and that is worth being able to see.
    transcript: String?

    -- The block this recording belongs to. A recording is not a floating asset:
    -- it lives somewhere in the writing, and that is how it can be found.
    placed_in: core/Block?

    -- Who spoke and when. Without authentication the speaker is the instance's
    -- owner by assumption, and the specification says so rather than pretending
    -- the claim is proven.
    evidence: core/OriginEvidence

    captured_by: core/Participant
    captured_at: Timestamp
}

-- What a block says about where it came from. It is a separate thing from the
-- block's content because content changes and origin does not — and it outlives
-- the audio, which is the whole reason it is separate from the recording too.
entity SpokenOrigin {
    block: core/Block
    recording: Recording
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Hablar dentro de un documento, en el punto donde se estaba escribiendo.
--
-- El bloque recibe la grabación y su denominación de origen en el mismo acto:
-- este bloque existe porque aquí se habló, y eso es un hecho desde el primer
-- instante, no algo que haya que declarar más tarde.
rule ParticipantSpeaksIntoABlock {
    when: CaptureVoiceInBlock(participant, graph, block, audio, media_type, evidence)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: graph, participant: participant}

    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    -- @invariant VoiceCanonicalityRequiresEvidence, from core: a recording that
    -- claims to be someone's voice arrives with what backs the claim.
    requires: evidence != null
    -- One block holds one recording. Two would leave it ambiguous which one the
    -- text came from.
    requires: not exists Recording{placed_in: block}

    ensures:
        let recording = Recording.created(
            graph: graph,
            audio: audio,
            media_type: media_type,
            transcript: null,
            placed_in: block,
            evidence: evidence,
            captured_by: participant,
            captured_at: now
        )
        SpokenOrigin.created(block: block, recording: recording)
}

-- Transcribir escribe el texto del bloque.
--
-- No deja una propuesta en un cajón aparte que alguien tenga que aceptar: deja
-- el texto donde el texto va, escrito por la máquina y firmado como tal. Desde
-- ese momento se edita como cualquier otro texto —se parte, se enlaza, se
-- reescribe— y en cuanto alguien lo toca, la autoría pasa a ser suya.
rule TranscribingWritesTheBlock {
    when: Transcribe(participant, recording, text)

    requires: recording.placed_in != null
    requires: text != ""

    ensures:
        recording.transcript = text
        -- Canal `authenticated_voice` con la evidencia de la grabación: es lo que
        -- el registro guardará de por vida sobre este texto.
        core/ParticipantSubmits(
            participant: participant,
            graph: recording.graph,
            origin_id: transcription_id(recording),
            change: edit_block(recording.placed_in, text),
            channel: authenticated_voice,
            evidence: recording.evidence
        )

    @guidance
        -- Volver a transcribir es esta misma regla otra vez: reemplaza el texto
        -- del bloque y el transcript de la grabación, y no toca el audio. Lo que
        -- se pierde es lo que hubiera escrito una persona encima, así que se
        -- advierte cuando el bloque ya no dice lo que la máquina había dejado.
}

-- Borrar el audio.
--
-- En cualquier momento, transcrito o no, y sólo cuando se pide exactamente eso.
-- La grabación no se borra: pierde su audio y lo dice. Lo que quedaba escrito
-- sigue escrito, y sigue nombrando de dónde vino.
rule ParticipantDiscardsAudio {
    when: DiscardAudio(participant, recording)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: recording.graph, participant: participant}

    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: recording.audio != null

    ensures: recording.audio = null

    @guidance
        -- Antes esto exigía haber recorrido una cascada entera. Exigirlo hacía a
        -- Vera dueña de una grabación que no es suya. Lo que corresponde es
        -- decir qué se va a perder —que si no hay transcripción es todo— y
        -- hacerlo cuando quien la tiene lo decida.
}

-- Un bloque al que le arrancan el sitio.
--
-- La grabación sobrevive. Borrar el bloque donde se hablaba no puede destruir lo
-- que ya se dijo: la grabación queda sin lugar y puede volver a colocarse.
rule DeletingAHeldBlockReleasesItsRecording {
    when: block/BlockDeleted(block)

    let recording = Recording{placed_in: block}

    requires: exists recording

    ensures: recording.placed_in = null
}

-- Partir contenido hablado deja las dos mitades habladas. Es la regla que hace
-- de @invariant OriginSurvivesFragmentation algo más que un deseo.
rule OriginFollowsASplit {
    when: block/BlockSplit(from, into)

    let origin = SpokenOrigin{block: from}

    requires: exists origin

    ensures: SpokenOrigin.created(block: into, recording: origin.recording)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant EveryRecordingCarriesItsEvidence {
    for r in Recordings:
        r.evidence != null
}

invariant NoOriginWithoutARecording {
    for o in SpokenOrigins:
        exists o.recording
}

-- Un lugar lo ocupa una sola grabación.
invariant APlaceIsHeldOnce {
    for r in Recordings:
        for other in Recordings:
            r.placed_in != null and other.placed_in = r.placed_in implies other = r
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Speaker {
    within: Recording
    identified_by: core/Participant
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface VoiceCapture {
    facing speaker: Speaker
    context recording: Recording

    exposes:
        recording.audio
        recording.transcript
        recording.evidence
        recording.captured_at
        recording.placed_in

    provides:
        CaptureVoiceInBlock(speaker, recording.graph, recording.placed_in, recording.audio, recording.media_type, recording.evidence)
        Transcribe(speaker, recording, text)
        DiscardAudio(speaker, recording)

    contracts:
        fulfils SpokenContent
        fulfils AppellationOfOrigin

    @guarantee TheRecordingIsAlwaysReachable
        -- While the audio exists, it is played from where its words are read,
        -- without opening anything. A recording one has to go and find is a
        -- recording one stops checking against.

    @guarantee AFinishedCaptureAppearsInItsBlock
        -- Stopping a recording replaces the recorder with the stored audio in
        -- the block where it was captured as soon as storage confirms it. The
        -- speaker does not navigate away and back, reload, or need a keyboard to
        -- make a recording that already exists in the corpus appear on screen.

    @guarantee ThreeThingsAndNoMore
        -- What a recording offers is: transcribe, transcribe again, discard. No
        -- step that must be completed, no state that must be advanced, nothing
        -- that has to be accepted before the writing can continue. Anything else
        -- is the ordinary editing of an ordinary block.

    @guarantee WhatWillBeLostIsSaidBeforeItIsLost
        -- Discarding audio that was never transcribed, and transcribing again
        -- over text a person has edited, both say what they are about to destroy.
        -- Neither is refused: they are someone's own words to keep or lose.

    @guarantee TranscribingDoesNotHoldTheSpeakerWaiting
        -- Asking for a transcript returns at once, and the recording says it is
        -- being transcribed until it is. A machine that thinks for minutes behind
        -- a request that must stay open is a machine that fails on the long
        -- recordings — which are exactly the ones worth transcribing.

    @guarantee AFinishedTranscriptAppearsWhereItWasRequested
        -- When transcription finishes, the recording and the written text
        -- appear together immediately on the open page. A device must not show
        -- the old action again while the words already exist in the corpus.

    @guarantee TheWholeRecordingIsTranscribed
        -- A finished transcript covers the recording from its beginning to its
        -- end. A long recording may take longer or be processed in parts, but
        -- Vera never presents an early fragment as the completed transcript.

    @guarantee SpeakingReachesTheDayFromAnywhere
        -- Recording is available wherever one is, and what is recorded without a
        -- stated place lands on the day in progress. Speaking is the mode that
        -- has to work with one hand while walking; asking where it should go
        -- first is asking at the worst possible moment. See daily-log.allium.

    @guarantee ActiveRecordingRemainsControllableAcrossNavigation
        -- While the microphone is open, duration, input level, destination,
        -- stop and discard remain visible over every page. Navigating cannot
        -- strand a live recording inside the page where it began.

    @guarantee NothingSpokenIsStrandedFromTheWriting
        -- A recording that lost its block can be found and placed again. A
        -- recording nobody can reach is a recording nobody can hear, and the
        -- evidence would exist without being evidence of anything.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "A transcript arrives as one block of text. Should transcribing offer to cut it — by pause, by sentence, by paragraph — or is cutting it the writer's own work, done by hand as they read it?"
open question "How does a block holding a recording appear in the Markdown mirror: as its text alone, as its text with a named reference to the audio, or as something a reader who never heard of Vera would recognise as having been spoken?"
open question "When authentication exists, does an unauthenticated recording keep the origin it was given under the assumption that the owner spoke?"
open question "Does a block whose spoken content was wholly rewritten still deserve to name the recording, or is there a point at which the appellation should lapse?"
open question "Transcribing again over edited text warns before replacing it. Should the text a person had written be kept anywhere, or is the warning the whole of the protection?"
